WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.770
Welcome to Module One: Agent Architecture and Design.

2
00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:08.445
Every agent system starts with one question: how much should the model decide?

3
00:00:09.245 --> 00:00:12.928
Picture a telecom support desk with fifty thousand tickets a month.

4
00:00:13.258 --> 00:00:17.027
Billing, plan changes, outages, and plenty of angry customers.

5
00:00:17.827 --> 00:00:21.275
Leadership sends a one-line request: add AI agents.

6
00:00:21.605 --> 00:00:24.414
Two teams take the job, and choose very differently.

7
00:00:25.214 --> 00:00:29.964
Team A goes big: five agents, including a supervisor to coordinate them.

8
00:00:30.294 --> 00:00:32.015
In the demo, it looks incredible.

9
00:00:32.815 --> 00:00:34.471
Production is a different story.

10
00:00:34.801 --> 00:00:37.752
Every handoff adds latency and re-sends context,

11
00:00:37.802 --> 00:00:40.127
so simple answers take twelve seconds.

12
00:00:40.927 --> 00:00:42.648
Costs swing from ticket to ticket.

13
00:00:42.978 --> 00:00:46.576
And when a refund goes wrong, nobody can tell which agent broke it.

14
00:00:47.376 --> 00:00:50.537
Four agents in a row, each ninety percent reliable,

15
00:00:50.587 --> 00:00:53.747
are only about sixty-six percent reliable together.

16
00:00:54.077 --> 00:00:55.990
Errors compound at every handoff.

17
00:00:56.790 --> 00:01:01.305
Team B asks a different question: what is the least agency that does the job?

18
00:01:01.635 --> 00:01:04.252
Most tickets, they notice, follow a few known paths.

19
00:01:05.052 --> 00:01:09.119
So a router sends each known ticket type to a fixed, testable workflow.

20
00:01:09.449 --> 00:01:12.855
Only open-ended cases reach a single agent with tools.

21
00:01:13.655 --> 00:01:16.549
The result is faster, cheaper, and easy to debug.

22
00:01:16.879 --> 00:01:19.901
When something fails, they know exactly which step to fix.

23
00:01:20.701 --> 00:01:23.924
That's this module's core idea: the agency spectrum,

24
00:01:23.974 --> 00:01:28.203
from fixed chains and routers to tool loops and multi-agent systems.

25
00:01:28.533 --> 00:01:33.347
Each step up buys flexibility, and pays in latency, cost, and predictability.

26
00:01:34.147 --> 00:01:38.257
You'll learn the loop every agent runs: reason, act, observe, repeat.

27
00:01:38.587 --> 00:01:41.503
Budgets and stop conditions make sure it always ends.

28
00:01:42.303 --> 00:01:45.008
Then four patterns built on that loop: ReAct,

29
00:01:45.058 --> 00:01:47.458
Plan and Execute, ReWOO, and Reflection.

30
00:01:47.788 --> 00:01:49.615
You'll see exactly when each one wins.

31
00:01:50.415 --> 00:01:53.352
Multi-agent systems aren't wrong, just expensive.

32
00:01:53.682 --> 00:01:58.432
They earn their cost through specialisation, context isolation, or parallel work.

33
00:01:59.232 --> 00:02:01.785
Anything else is one agent wearing a costume.

34
00:02:02.115 --> 00:02:05.755
Splitting it up only adds handoffs, tokens, and places to fail.

35
00:02:06.555 --> 00:02:09.005
This domain is fifteen percent of the exam,

36
00:02:09.055 --> 00:02:12.201
and it rewards reading constraints before capabilities.

37
00:02:12.531 --> 00:02:16.074
Sub-second, regulated, or fixed process means less agency,

38
00:02:16.124 --> 00:02:18.241
and that's where lesson one begins.
